WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:06.660
- There are a bunch of islands
that the United States owns

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.890
at the very edge of this
map, deep into the Pacific

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:09.723
like out here.

00:00:09.723 --> 00:00:12.410
And they all say like a
little U.S. next to them.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:14.910
They're teeny tiny
island where no one lives

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:16.800
and they're just in the
middle of the Pacific

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.410
so far away from the actual United States.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:21.270
Why do we own all these weird islands?

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:22.250
What happened?

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:26.657
Well, it has to do with
farming and bird poop.

00:00:26.657 --> 00:00:29.907
(upbeat hiphop music)

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.057
Back in the old days,
farming looked like this.

00:00:38.057 --> 00:00:40.590
(cows mooing)

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:44.090
Basically just small groups of
people usually a few families

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.510
that would grow food for their community

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:48.110
and that was about it.

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.470
But soon the world started to change

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:53.240
and started to look a lot less like this

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.130
and a lot more like this.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.140
People started moving into the city

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.620
and working in factories,

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:01.780
mass producing commodities and items

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:03.810
that would be shipped
all around the world.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.420
And suddenly farmers had an incentive

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.320
and a duty to make more food

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.270
to ship it to these non-farm workers

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:12.620
who are now living in cities.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:17.620
Farms started specializing in
one crop like corn or wheat

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:19.230
and they were able to sell their crops

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:21.650
not only to their local markets

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:24.600
but to markets all around the world.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.400
But there's a problem here,

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.070
when you're a farmer and
you're producing the same crop

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.900
year after year, your soil
starts to lose its nutrients.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.010
It doesn't have time to regenerate

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.350
and it eventually becomes
completely exhausted

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.790
making it impossible for
you to produce in abundance.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:44.000
And if you can't make food in abundance

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.230
then you can't feed
these ever-growing cities

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.340
of people who are working in factories.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:51.670
And then the global economy
starts to break down

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:53.333
and the whole thing stops.

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:56.180
For a moment there in the 1800's

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:57.510
it was looking like earth

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:01.080
would not be able to sustain
a population over like

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:02.930
two and a half billion people

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:05.810
because there wasn't enough
nutrients in the soil

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.550
to keep up with the demand for food.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.110
But luckily there was a solution

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.560
to this soil nutrients problem.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.260
And that solution looked like this.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:16.843
(upbeat music)

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:19.050
- One oh!

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.980
- Animal waste from any animal

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:24.020
is packed with the nutrients
that the soil needs

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:28.090
to rejuvenate itself to grow
more crops year after year.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.330
Another term for this is fertilizer.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:31.890
And so now we're talking about farms

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.180
and industrialization and fertilizer

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:36.740
and you're probably thinking
this is a boring story

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:39.280
but these simple facts
led the United States

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.750
to expand well beyond

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:44.780
the borders that we think
of the United States today,

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.110
to colonize far flung islands

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:49.210
in the Pacific and the Caribbean

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:53.914
all in search of one special
commodity, bird shit.

00:02:53.914 --> 00:02:56.581
(upbeat music)

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.500
So it's the mid 1800's and like I said,

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.940
these farmers are growing
a ton of a single crop

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.820
sending it mainly to
cities to be consumed.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.260
Now, before all of this urbanization

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.240
when people would eat this food,

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.090
the waste that they would produce

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.990
would be captured and
brought back onto the field

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:17.220
which would help rejuvenate
the nutrients in the field

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:19.510
and allow them to continue to grow

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:21.530
in this sort of sustainable cycle.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:23.500
But now all of that human waste

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.030
was being piped into sewage systems

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:29.210
and then sent out to
rivers and to the ocean

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:30.660
completely lost.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:31.670
One estimate said that

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:35.730
every year $50 million
worth of valuable fertilizer

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:39.080
was just wasted away
through the sewer system.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:40.645
And this is where the bird shit comes in.

00:03:40.645 --> 00:03:44.380
(birds chirping)
(upbeat music)

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:47.960
If you go down to Peru and
look right off the coast

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.790
you're gonna see a few islands.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:53.160
These are a little more
than just some rocks

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:56.060
in the middle of the ocean
that were totally uninhabited.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:57.520
Well, I guess they were inhabited

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.713
just not by humans but by birds.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.870
These birds spend their day eating fish

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:05.800
from the ocean around the rocks,

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:07.600
inevitably dropping their waste,

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:08.780
pair this with the fact that

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:10.560
these rocks don't get a lot of rain.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.890
So instead this bird dung just sits there

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:16.320
day after day, year after
year baking in the sun

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.930
and eventually it becomes like rock hard.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.730
You can imagine that
after thousands of years

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:25.760
of this process you start to
get some pretty thick layers

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:26.780
of bird shit.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.690
These deposits would get up to 60 meters.

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:31.940
That's like the size
of this random building

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:33.960
in Argentina that I found on the internet.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.250
The building's not
important, the height is.

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:40.860
This calcified bird poop is called guano

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.000
and it contains exactly

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.840
what the farmers in the
United States needed

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:49.430
to continue with the demand
for more and more food

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:50.320
in the cities.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.640
Because again, bird and
animal waste is full of

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:55.600
chemical properties like nitrogen

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:58.410
that are vital for regenerating soil,

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:00.140
meaning they're good fertilizer.

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.560
Once this guano was discovered,

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.330
there was a race for
everyone around the world

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:06.580
to get their hands on it.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:09.670
Soon in the United States,

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:13.320
guano became this like
mythical magical substance.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.010
There's all these tall tales that pop up

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:18.150
where like this dad
leaves his kid in the shed

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:19.690
where the guano is contained

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:21.620
and he opens the shed an hour later

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:24.710
and the kid is now a full
grown man with facial hair

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:27.780
or the farmer who sprinkles
guano on his cucumbers

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.340
and the vines of the cucumbers pop up

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:32.530
and start to like attack the man.

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:33.750
I mean, it's just crazy.

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:36.070
It's obviously not fact-based or true

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:38.170
but it gave a sense that
there was this craze

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:41.020
that guano was going to be the savior

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:43.570
of all of the farmers
issues in the United States.

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:47.780
So inevitably an economy and
a trade around guano emerged.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:51.120
And as always at this time,
the British got there first.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:52.320
So there was a British firm

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.640
that had a monopoly
over all the guano trade

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:57.030
from these islands in Peru,

00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:58.690
which was sort of the best known place

00:05:58.690 --> 00:05:59.710
for guano at the time.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:01.930
So with this one British firm controlling

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:05.480
the whole guano trade and demand surging,

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:07.000
the prices skyrocketed

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.750
making it way more expensive
for American farmers

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:12.570
to get their hands on this amazing stuff.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:15.190
So who steps in to solve this issue?

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:16.940
The president of the United States.

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:19.310
In his first state of the union address,

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:22.730
President Fillmore gets
up and talks about guano.

00:06:22.730 --> 00:06:24.027
He says that,

00:06:24.027 --> 00:06:27.410
"Peruvian guano has become
so desirable an article"

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:30.350
that he felt that "the
duty of the government

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:34.140
to secure it at a good
price for American farmers."

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:35.800
This is crazy to me.

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:38.320
Can you imagine the president
of the United States

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:40.280
getting up to address the nation

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.300
and making it a national priority

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.600
to go to some far flung place

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:49.100
to secure some natural resource that

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:51.480
is really important for economic growth

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:52.830
- On my orders,

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:55.560
coalition forces have began
striking selected targets

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:59.520
of military importance to
undermine Saddam Hussein's ability

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:00.860
to wage war.

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:03.193
- Yeah, I mean it's
not that weird I guess.

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:07.980
So yeah this is becoming
a geopolitical issue

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:11.270
the president is involved,
they want all the guano.

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:15.650
President Fillmore says, "nothing
will be omitted on my part

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:20.040
in the effort to secure this
magical life giving bird shit."

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:22.283
Okay actually he didn't say that last part

00:07:22.283 --> 00:07:26.970
the quotation should
have ended, like up here.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:29.180
I just sort of paraphrased the rest.

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:30.450
The point is President Fillmore

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:32.110
was serious about this issue.

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:34.890
He was gonna get his guano at any cost.

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:38.240
So he did what you do when
you're a rising superpower

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:40.810
and he promised to send in warships

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:42.750
to these Peruvian Islands if he had to

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:45.090
in order to secure guano at a good price,

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:47.670
even though Peru
rightfully had sovereignty

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:48.950
over these islands

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:53.180
and yet here's a president
sending in warships

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:55.870
in order to secure the resource

00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:58.660
again, kind of sounds familiar.

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:00.740
- America is addicted to oil.

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:02.760
- But American lawmakers in Congress

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:05.480
had another answer to this problem

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:09.410
which would allow Americans to
get their own Guano Islands.

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:12.350
The proposed law said
that any American citizen

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:15.640
not a government official
or a military personnel

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:19.290
could go out on their
boat anywhere in the ocean

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.910
and if they stumble
upon one of these rocks

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:26.510
that's covered in calcified
bird poop they could claim it

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:27.690
not just for themselves

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:30.230
but for the country of the United States.

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:34.700
No treaties, no negotiations,
no official declaration,

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:37.120
no government involvement even

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:40.460
just American citizens out on their boats,

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:42.123
looking for bird poop islands.

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:45.450
Some lawmakers at the time were

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:48.140
understandably uneasy with this law.

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:49.320
Now remember that the U.S.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:52.210
was expanding a lot at this time.

00:08:52.210 --> 00:08:54.190
Remember that video I made about Mexico

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:57.520
and how they're sort taking
over Texas and California

00:08:57.520 --> 00:08:58.430
and that whole area.

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:00.340
Yeah, that's happening around this time.

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:04.090
But the U.S. hadn't moved
beyond this landmass,

00:09:04.090 --> 00:09:06.670
this contiguous shape that we think of

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:08.510
when we think of the United States.

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:09.980
Yeah, we had moved West

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:13.370
but we hadn't gone for far-flung colonies

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:15.280
far into the Pacific ocean.

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:17.470
And secondly, the lawmakers are like,

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:21.360
since when do we let just
like random American citizens

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:24.180
go out and like help us colonize islands

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:25.470
anywhere in the world.

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:29.170
This would set a precedent that
these lawmakers were worried

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:31.500
would have way bigger consequences

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:33.920
than just like letting
people go get fertilizer

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:35.000
for their farms.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:36.080
The sponsor of this bill

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:38.360
was trying to get it through Congress,

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:40.780
came out and said, "listen
guys, you're overreacting.

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:42.800
This isn't like legit imperialism.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:44.040
We're not like going out

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:45.740
and like taking over whole countries

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:48.220
like the British or the French were doing

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:50.490
carving up continents, no, no no.

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:53.480
We're just going to these
rocks to get some fertilizer

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:55.170
for our farms it's harmless."

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:58.490
And this was apparently
enough to convince the critics

00:09:58.490 --> 00:09:59.990
and this law passes.

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:03.330
The Guano Islands Act of 1856

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:05.030
where literally any American

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:07.890
can go help expand the United States

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:11.370
by claiming Guano Islands
and mining this stuff

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:13.040
and bringing it back to the United States.

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:15.290
And so now a giant blitz

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:19.157
of guano exploration begins
by American citizens.

00:10:19.157 --> 00:10:22.030
(upbeat music)

00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:23.340
Sort of like the gold rush,

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:25.793
but you know, for bird poop.

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:32.710
Soon this claim start rolling
in to the U.S. government

00:10:32.710 --> 00:10:35.410
random guy X from Nebraska,

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:38.870
sailed to a random island in the Pacific

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:40.580
planted an American flag

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:43.450
and now it belongs to the
United States of America.

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:46.980
All in all by the end there
were 94 separate islands

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:49.690
mainly in the Pacific and the Caribbean.

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:53.620
The guano was chipped away,
harvested, put on ships,

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:55.230
brought back to the United States

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:57.073
and farmers thrived.

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:00.870
But you know who didn't thrive?

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:03.120
The people who had to mine this stuff.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:04.360
I mean, you can imagine

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:07.040
being the person who has
to go onto these islands

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:10.580
where you have thousands
and thousands of years

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:13.320
worth of layers of bird droppings

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:15.850
to drill and cut that away

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:17.740
and to bring it onto a ship

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:19.270
to be shipped back to United States

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:21.840
it's just like excruciating
to think about.

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:23.640
This was a pretty horrendous job.

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:26.070
And it came with all sorts of side effects

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:29.420
like lung issues or running out of food

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:31.230
because you're not on an
island that grows food

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:32.630
you're on a literal like rock

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:34.730
and so the food would
go bad in these ships

00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:37.220
and the people would be
like stranded at sea.

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:41.500
Unsurprisingly these guano
barons would trick people

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:44.030
into coming with them
on these expeditions.

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:45.060
In the case of the Pacific

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:47.960
they would often target Hawaiians
to recruit for these jobs.

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:49.140
And in the case of the Caribbean

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:51.330
they would often target Black Americans

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:52.930
recently freed from slavery

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:55.220
to come work on these guano ships.

00:11:55.220 --> 00:11:57.860
As some members of Congress
at the time worried,

00:11:57.860 --> 00:12:00.420
the Guano Act had ramifications

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:03.130
well beyond guano and fertilizer.

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:06.580
In fact, guano became totally
obsolete a few years later

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:10.810
in the early 1900's when
a German chemist invented

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:13.400
perhaps the most important
invention of all time,

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:16.090
which was synthetic fertilizer

00:12:16.090 --> 00:12:17.760
a way to actually make fertilizer

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:20.180
without having to get it
from like animal waste.

00:12:20.180 --> 00:12:24.150
This allowed the population
to grow to what it is today

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:27.080
and really changed the
history of humanity.

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:30.000
But what it did is it made guano obsolete.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.940
And yet this law that allows Americans

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:34.050
to take over random islands

00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:37.280
and the islands themselves
remained on the books.

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:38.550
But eventually with time,

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:41.910
these claims for random rocks
in the Pacific and Caribbean

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:45.650
sort of just withered away and
we either withdrew the claims

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:46.890
or people forgot about them

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:49.420
and they were eventually
claimed by local countries

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:51.760
like Kiribati or Tonga

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:53.970
and places like Haiti in the Caribbean.

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:56.030
But a few of these Guano Islands

00:12:56.030 --> 00:12:58.440
that we originally claimed remain today.

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:00.670
We still own them, they're
still a part of our borders

00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:02.790
in both the Pacific and the Caribbean

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:05.010
but the U.S. government isn't
interested in using them

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:06.840
for guano anymore obviously,

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:09.810
these mostly uninhabited
rocks in the ocean

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:11.940
are mainly used by the U.S. military

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:15.070
for landing strips and sea ports.

00:13:15.070 --> 00:13:18.270
They also serve to help the
U.S. with their ocean borders

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:19.960
because international law says that

00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:21.630
if you have a piece of island,

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:25.210
you get a big swath of ocean around it

00:13:25.210 --> 00:13:27.190
for your exclusive economic zone.

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:29.260
Meaning the country who owns this island

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:31.470
has exclusive economic rights

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:33.570
in all of this ocean around their islands.

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:36.670
So this greatly enhances
the U.S.'s ability

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:37.930
to have sovereignty and control

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:39.300
over certain parts of the ocean

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:40.960
in the Pacific and Caribbean.

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:45.960
But perhaps the most important
result from this guano fest

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:48.060
was a psychological one.

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:49.490
Like we talked about before,

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:52.780
the U.S. was expanding
but it hadn't expanded

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:56.680
beyond this contiguous
land mass in North America

00:13:56.680 --> 00:13:59.110
that it was taking over
rapidly in the 1800's.

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:01.320
But soon after this laws passed,

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:05.740
suddenly the U.S. had
possessions in far away oceans.

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:10.740
And what happens after
this is a domino effect.

00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:13.780
Suddenly if we can take
over islands in the Pacific,

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:16.070
why can't we annex Hawaii?

00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:18.200
Or occupy Cuba?

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:19.380
Or Puerto Rico?

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:22.280
Or take over an entire
country of the Philippines

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:23.410
from Spain?

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:25.500
This law that allowed Americans

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:28.330
to claim islands all over the world

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:33.330
was the first step towards
real big boy empire behavior.

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:39.040
And it all started with
the hunt for bird shit.

